    "incident_title": "Contacts marked UNREACHABLE without their phones ever ringing",
    "outcome": "conclusive",
    "summary": "Neither a carrier outage nor the 80-channel limit is the cause. Outbound call attempts exceeded the carrier's contracted 15 call-attempts/sec (all INVITEs counted). The carrier rejected the excess INVITEs with SIP 429 (about 71% of INVITEs on 10 March). The dialer treats each 429 as a failed attempt and retries after a fixed 120s. After 4 such failures the scheduler marks the contact UNREACHABLE, even though the call was never placed. So the contacts were marked UNREACHABLE without their phones ringing. Our own scheduler is the source of the excess: it dispatches new attempts at a configured 12/sec, and retries are added on top of that with no shared trunk-wide cap. Observed sustained load was about 990 INVITEs/min (~16.5/sec), above the 15/sec contract. Campaign K-2214 (21,000 contacts) started at 04:00 UTC on 10 March, and the 429 onset at ~05:00 coincides with its ramp-up. As of the last data point (12:00 UTC, 10 March) about 9,440 contacts had been marked UNREACHABLE, and the 429 ratio was still ~0.71.",
    "root_cause": "Our outbound dispatch (campaign-scheduler new attempts at 12/sec plus retries, with no global cap against the carrier's 15 attempts/sec contract) exceeded the SIP trunk's call-attempt rate. The carrier answered the excess INVITEs with 429. The dialer counted each 429 as a failed attempt, and after 4 attempts the scheduler marked the contacts UNREACHABLE without ever placing a call.",
